  "@type": "Note",
  "@id": "note:notion-78",
  "title": "Chris Seager",
  "author": "person:jane-brew:001",
  "status": "draft",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "b-1",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Chris Seager"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-2",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Chris Seager's cinematography style is characterized by several unique aspects:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-3",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "1. Artistic focus: Seager emphasizes that cinematography is fundamentally about art. While he acknowledges the importance of technology, he believes in pushing the limits of whatever tools he has at his disposal to achieve his artistic vision.[1]"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-4",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "2. Expressive techniques: Seager is known for pushing the boundaries of his medium, whether it's film or digital. He experiments with techniques like overexposure, double-exposure, and other methods to create expressive and unique visual effects. His goal is to make the medium a tool for his artistic expression, rather than being constrained by its limitations.[1]"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-5",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "3. Versatility in genre: Seager excels in adapting his style to different genres, particularly fantasy and period pieces. He enjoys working on projects that allow him to explore dark, Victorian, and quirky themes. His approach often involves starting with a dark canvas and then selectively lighting scenes to enhance the storytelling, which he finds particularly exciting as a cinematographer.[1]"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-6",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "4. Period perfection with a noir twist: In period pieces like \"A Kind of Murder,\" Seager demonstrates his ability to create visually rich, colorful worlds while incorporating noirish lighting and lensing. He uses color strategically, with reds, blues, and purples often carrying significance. His work is described as moody and saturated, skillfully balancing light and shadow.[2]"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-7",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "5. Hitchcockian influence: Seager often draws inspiration from classic filmmakers like Alfred Hitchcock. He employs a deceptively simple style of camerawork that gives actors room to perform while using precise framing and camera angles to create tension and ambiguity. In some projects, he deliberately uses static camera work to convey emotional states, such as depicting a \"stillborn, loveless, broken marriage\" through an unmoving camera in domestic scenes.[2]"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-8",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "6. Distinctive lighting techniques: Seager uses lighting to differentiate between social classes and settings. For example, in \"Carnival Row,\" he employed a cold daylight look interspersed with firelight for poorer areas, while using warmer, more contrasted lighting for wealthier districts. This approach extends to interiors, with smaller windows and colder light for poorer settings, and larger windows allowing more light in affluent areas.[5]"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-9",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "These elements combine to create Seager's unique visual style, which is both technically proficient and artistically expressive, adapting to the needs of each project while maintaining a distinctive aesthetic approach."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-10",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Citations:"
        }
      ]
